            <ArticleTitle>OCULAR AND OSTEOARTICULAR TUBERCULOSIS IN A YOUNG PATIENT. CASE REPORT.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>123-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CASE REPORT" NlmCategory="METHODS">A young healthy patient, health-care worker in a state hospital, presented in the eye department complaining of pain and blurred vision in the left eye for approx. 2 weeks. Examination revealed a VA of 12/20 in the left eye, an interstitial keratitis, some signs of vitreal inflammation and two chorioretinal mass lesions (at echography appearing cystic) in the affected eye. She also mentioned a chronic pain in the right wrist. No systemic association was found. Based on the orthopaedic examination, biopsy, and surgical intervention, a strong suspicion of ocular tuberculosis was made and the patient was advised to start tuberculostatic treatment for 12 months and ocular steroidian treatment for 4 months. The ocular manifestations regressed totally after 3 months of treatment, the VA of the left eye improving at 20/ 20.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Tuberculosis can present many manifestations, with multi systemic involvement. Ocular tuberculosis is a difficult diagnosis and thus requires thorough multi-disciplinary investigations.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
